  "query_preview": ".. \nIssued at 337 AM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nBroad upper level ridge across the southern US is going to lead to \nheat building into the region for at least the first half of the \nweek; however, a series of disturbances will challenge the potential \nof reaching advisory level heat Monday - Wednesday.  A strong low \nlevel jet has developed across the Central Plains extending from the \npanhandle of TX into southwest Iowa.  This has lead to the \ndevelopment of elevated storms rooted somewhere near 700 mb.  These \nstorms are largely expected to remain sub severe through the morning \nhours, pushing southeast in time.  Rainfall amounts are expected to \nbe generally fairly light, less than a quarter of an inch, but in \nareas where there is some training, could see rainfall amounts \napproach an inch.  Storms should largely push southeast into the \nmorning hours and weaken after sunrise leading to gradual clearing.  \n\n00Z soundings from both SGF and LZK displayed abundant moisture\nin the near surface layer. Elevated showers this morning should\nalso provide additional fuel leading to muggy conditions with \nsolar heating today. With expected high temperatures in the 90s \nand dewpoints in the 70s, expect heat indices to warm towards \n105 F this afternoon. Have coordinated a heat advisory \nregionwide for today, with the exception of central Missouri \nwhere limited relief from the heat is expected until mid-week. \nAs a result, have an excessive heat warning beginning today and \ncontinuing into Wednesday evening.\n\nShortwave building east along the northern periphery of the upper \nridge may interact with the low level jet again tonight leading to \nmore elevated showers and storms across the region. Residual cloud \ncover/elevated showers on Monday leads to uncertainty of high \ntemperatures across northern Missouri, and as a result, don't have \nheat headlines across northern Missouri beyond today- despite the \npotential. \n\nAn additional wave on Tuesday leads to the potential for additional \nthunderstorms across northern Missouri.  Where the convection does \nnot develop, hot and humid conditions are expected to persist (most \nlikely across central MO).  Northern Missouri remains outlooked \nTuesday and Wednesday for the potential of a strong storm or two.\n\nA stationary boundary to the north is expected to push south on \nWednesday leading to more temperate and seasonal conditions through \nthe latter half of the week.",
